doc.title='Emerald Housing — Architecture, Runtime, and Interview Guide';
doc.head.append(el('meta',{name:'description',content:'The complete Emerald Housing project handbook: business scope, Java, APIs, data, Redis execution, resilience, permissions, Kubernetes, operations, practical labs, and interviews.'}));
doc.body.innerHTML=`<a class="skip" href="#main-content">Skip to the guide</a><div id="reading-progress" aria-hidden="true"></div><header class="topbar"><a class="wordmark" href="#overview"><span class="brand-icon" aria-hidden="true">E</span><span>Emerald Housing<small>Architecture · Runtime · Interview</small></span></a><div class="top-actions"><button id="theme" type="button" class="button">Dark theme</button><button id="menubtn" type="button" class="button menu-button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="side">Contents</button><button id="print-document" type="button" class="button print-button">Print / PDF</button></div></header><div class="shell"><aside id="side" class="sidebar" aria-label="Guide navigation"><div class="search-area"><label for="doc-search">Find an explanation</label><input id="doc-search" type="search" placeholder="Try Redis, permissions, ETag…" aria-controls="doc-search-results" autocomplete="off"><p class="search-hint">Press / to search · Esc to close</p><p id="doc-search-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><div id="doc-search-results" hidden></div></div><nav id="chapter-navigation" aria-label="Chapters"><div class="nav-shortcuts"><a href="#overview">Guide overview</a><a href="#interview-learning-path">Reading paths</a><a href="#interview-short-answers">Quick revision</a></div><div class="nav-heading"><span>12 chapters</span><button id="toc-toggle-all" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="toc-chapters">Expand all</button></div><div id="toc-chapters"></div><a class="sources-link" href="#markdown-documents">Source index and examples</a></nav><p id="toc-current" class="reading-location" aria-live="polite">Start with the project overview.</p></aside><main id="main-content"><header id="overview" class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">A complete guide to one working system</p><h1>From a request<br>to the reason behind it.</h1><p class="hero-description">Learn Emerald Housing through its business rules, service boundaries, runtime flows, and design choices. Use the same guide to read the code, run the project, and prepare your interview explanation.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="primary-link" href="#interview-chapter-story">Start with the project <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="#redis-runtime-walkthrough">Understand the two Redis instances</a></div><div class="hero-facts"><span><strong>3</strong> Java applications</span><span><strong>2</strong> service-owned databases</span><span><strong>2</strong> Redis workloads</span><span><strong>1</strong> society in scope</span></div></header><section id="interview-learning-path" class="reading-paths"><div class="section-kicker">How to read this guide</div><h2 id="interview-learning-path-title">Choose your starting point.</h2><div class="path-grid"><a href="#interview-chapter-story"><span>Learn the project</span><strong>Read the chapters in order.</strong><small>Scope → implementation → runtime → operations.</small></a><a href="#interview-short-answers"><span>Prepare for an interview</span><strong>Start with the short answers.</strong><small>Revise the story, then investigate the follow-up questions.</small></a><a href="#interview-chapter-lab"><span>Work hands on</span><strong>Run one workflow at a time.</strong><small>Choose a runtime, authenticate, call an API, and inspect evidence.</small></a></div><p class="reading-note">Each chapter gives the explanation first, then groups the implementation references and scenario questions beside that topic. Configuration describes the repository; recorded verification results describe a particular run.</p></section></main></div><footer class="site-footer">Emerald Housing · Repository handbook · Reorganized 25 October 2026<br>Current explanations, detailed implementation references, and dated verification evidence are identified separately.</footer><button id="back-top" class="button back-top" type="button" hidden>↑ Back to top</button><div id="guide-announcement" class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>`;
const main=doc.querySelector('main');
const makeTable=(heads,rows)=>`<div class="tablebox"><table><thead><tr>${heads.map(h=>`<th scope="col">${h}</th>`).join('')}</tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(row=>`<tr>${row.map(cell=>`<td>${cell}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>`;
const note=(title,body)=>`<aside class="callout"><strong>${title}</strong><p>${body}</p></aside>`;
const flow=(label,steps)=>`<ol class="runtime-flow" aria-label="${label}">${steps.map(([title,body])=>`<li><strong>${title}</strong><p>${body}</p></li>`).join('')}</ol>`;
const code=(text)=>`<pre><code>${esc(text)}</code></pre>`;
const narratives=[
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-project-pitch"><h3>The business problem</h3><p>Emerald Housing manages the people and homes in the EMERALD society. Society Service owns the society, floors, and flats. Member Service owns members, flat-membership history, accounts, and access administration. Gateway is the common HTTP entry point. This division keeps the owner of each business record clear.</p><p>The repository also supports registered ownership, family relationships, scoped resident access, audit history, searchable directories, and conditional updates. The detailed feature and permission inventories below explain their rules. Society and floor data are seeded; the current application is scoped to one society.</p><h3>A project explanation you can say aloud</h3><blockquote>“I built a Java service-based housing application. Society owns building data, Member owns people and assignments, and Gateway handles the entry path and sessions. Each service owns its database. We use Redis for shared sessions, rate limits, membership locks, and disposable Society caches. Database constraints, scoped permissions, auditing, and bounded dependency calls protect the important workflows.”</blockquote><p>When discussing your contribution, name the part you implemented and explain one request from entry to persistence. Use the source references to support the details you claim.</p><h3>Understand the users before the technology</h3>${makeTable(['Actor or concept','Meaning in this application','Boundary to explain'],[['Administrator','Maintains permitted business records and application access.','Administrative authority is checked by the relevant operation.'],['Committee user','Has the grants assigned to its enabled role.','A role name is not a substitute for checking permissions.'],['Resident','Uses self or owned-flat scopes linked to a member.','Being authenticated does not grant access to every record.'],['Membership','A dated association between a member and a flat.','History and current eligibility are different concerns.'],['Registered owner and family','Business relationships used by the newer access workflows.','Ownership, family membership, and account identity are related but distinct.']])}${note('Current scope','Multi-society onboarding, billing, and database sharding are design extensions rather than completed features. Treat each extension as a separate requirement and migration project.')}</section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="java-interview-summary"><h3>What Spring Boot provides—and what your code must provide</h3><p>Spring Boot configures the application around the dependencies and properties you supply. It wires HTTP infrastructure, persistence, validation, caching, security integrations, and operational endpoints. It does not choose the business rules, permission scopes, transaction boundaries, or safe retry policy for you.</p><p>The project uses Java 21. Records carry request and response values, streams map data, and lambdas supply behavior to existing APIs. These choices make the code smaller when they fit the task; they do not make database calls asynchronous or business state deeply immutable.</p>${makeTable(['Language or framework choice','Use it to explain','Do not claim'],[['Records','Compact API carriers; constructors can validate or defensively copy values.','A record automatically makes nested mutable objects immutable.'],['Collection factories and copies','Return fixed values or a shallow defensive snapshot.','Every stream collector returns a mutable ArrayList.'],['Streams and method references','Map results, construct orders, and make transformations explicit.','Sorting a Java list replaces database ordering and pagination.'],['Comparator and Comparable','Separate an external ordering strategy from a type’s natural order.','Every domain class must implement Comparable.'],['WebFlux','Gateway uses reactive HTTP infrastructure.','The MVC/JPA backends become nonblocking because Gateway is reactive.'],['Virtual threads','Understand where a configured executor is used and how it is closed.','More threads remove connection-pool or downstream capacity limits.']])}<h3>Read a backend request through its layers</h3>${flow('Spring request execution',[['Transport and identity','Filters establish the validated request identity and enforce transport-level protections.'],['Controller and DTO validation','HTTP inputs are parsed and checked before the application operation is invoked.'],['Service and authorization','The service applies the permission and business checks appropriate to the operation.'],['Persistence and transaction','Repository calls use the service-owned database and the transaction currently in effect.'],['Response and operations','The result is mapped to an API representation; logs, metrics, and tracing provide operational context.']])}<p>Keep request authorization and transactional work within their intended execution context. Moving work to another executor needs an explicit lifecycle and context strategy.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-microservice-boundaries"><h3>Three applications with separate responsibilities</h3>${makeTable(['Application','Owns or coordinates','Dependency'],[['API Gateway','Routing, authenticated sessions, CSRF, and request rate limits.','Member credential/identity validation and main Redis.'],['Society Service','Society, floors, flat records, and relevant audits.','society_db and the dedicated cache Redis.'],['Member Service','Members, membership history, application accounts, access, and family workflows.','member_db, Society validation, and main Redis.']])}<p>Database per service means that service replicas share their service’s data store while other services use its API. It does not mean one database for every Pod, nor does it require every database to run on a separate physical machine.</p><h3>Follow a membership assignment</h3>${flow('Membership assignment',[['Gateway receives the request','The request must have a valid session and satisfy the applicable CSRF and rate-limit checks. Routing sends it to Member Service.'],['Member checks authority and eligibility','The assignment operation requires its configured permission. The member must exist and be active. The current implementation also coordinates access changes with a database-backed registry lock.'],['Member asks Society about the flat','An authenticated internal lookup obtains the authoritative flat state; this validation deliberately avoids the disposable response cache. An inactive flat is not eligible.'],['The assignment is coordinated','A Redis lock serializes membership operations for the same member. Persistence rechecks state under a database row lock, rejects a duplicate active relationship, and relies on the database constraint as a final guard.'],['The business change and audit are committed','MembershipWriter persists the association and records the audit in the transaction. The returned result describes the saved relationship.']])}${note('An important transaction detail','The current assign method itself is transactional and takes the access-registry lock before the Society lookup. Do not describe this implementation as always making the network call outside a short database transaction. Narrower transaction boundaries would be a separate improvement.') }<h3>What this flow does not guarantee</h3><p>There is no cross-service database transaction. Society can change after validation and before Member commits. Explain the accepted consistency window and the compensating or asynchronous design you would introduce if the business required stronger coordination.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="api-client-guide"><h3>Use the contract for the operation you are calling</h3><p>Start from the saved Postman collection and controller mappings. Gateway routing covers society, floor, flat, member, membership, access, account, owner, and family paths. Route order matters: the Member-owned flat-membership path must be matched before the general Society flat path.</p>${makeTable(['Caller concern','What to do','Why it matters'],[['Authentication','Obtain CSRF material, log in with a private account, then use the resulting session cookie.','Session state and CSRF protection are separate mechanisms.'],['Authorization','Use an account with the operation’s permission and the correct self, owned-flat, or society scope.','The same route can return different permitted records for different identities.'],['Conditional updates','Read the current representation and ETag; send the supported If-Match value when editing.','A stale editor should not silently overwrite a newer change.'],['Idempotent creates','Use the supported idempotency key and keep the request meaning stable when retrying.','A replay must not accidentally create another member.'],['Directory queries','Use supported filters, bounded page sizes, and allowed sort fields.','Inputs must preserve safe and deterministic database queries.'],['Cursor queries','Return the issued cursor with the same applicable query and authorization context.','A cursor is continuation state, not an unrestricted list position.']])}<h3>Read errors as part of the interface</h3><p>A successful HTTP connection does not imply a successful business operation. Separate invalid input, missing authentication, denied access, a missing resource, a conflict or stale version, an exceeded limit, and an unavailable dependency. The detailed API reference and saved requests show the operation-specific status and response fields.</p><h3>Compatibility choices are configuration choices</h3><p>The documented compatibility mode accepts updates without If-Match. The stricter setting is <code>APP_HTTP_REQUIRE_IF_MATCH=true</code> in both backends. Turn it on only with clients that send current validators. Cursor and page-number APIs also serve different client needs; keep their scope and ordering rules explicit.</p><p class="source-note">Runnable contract: <a href="postman/Emerald-Housing-Option-B.postman_collection.json">Option B Postman collection</a> · <a href="postman/Emerald-Housing-Option-B.postman_environment.json">Environment template</a>. Credential values belong in private local variables.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-data-correctness"><h3>Give every correctness mechanism a specific job</h3>${makeTable(['Mechanism','Problem it addresses','Limit'],[['Database ownership','One service controls its business tables and migrations.','It does not provide a distributed transaction.'],['Local transaction','Related business and audit writes commit or roll back together.','Its guarantees stop at that database boundary.'],['Pessimistic row lock','Serializes competing operations on the selected member row.','Lock wait and transaction duration consume capacity.'],['Database unique constraint','Rejects a duplicate active membership even if earlier checks race.','The application must translate the violation into a useful response.'],['Optimistic version / ETag','Detects an edit based on an older representation.','The client must handle a conflict and refresh.'],['Idempotency record','Recognizes a retry of a supported create operation.','Reusing a key for a different request is a conflict.'],['Redis member lock','Coordinates protected member operations across replicas.','A lease is bounded; database integrity remains essential.']])}<h3>Explain one concrete race</h3><p>Two requests can both read that no active membership exists. An application-level existence check alone cannot guarantee uniqueness. The row lock coordinates the relevant write sequence; the unique constraint remains the final decision at persistence. Returning a business conflict is more useful than exposing a raw integrity exception.</p><h3>Queries and connection budgets</h3><p>DTO projections can avoid unnecessary entity loading, but query count and query plans must be measured. Choose indexes for actual filters, ordering, and selectivity. A composite index has an order; adding every searchable column is not a capacity plan.</p><p>Each Pod has its own connection pool. The potential application connection demand is the sum of replica counts multiplied by the corresponding pool limits, plus other database clients and operational headroom. Autoscaling the service can therefore increase database pressure even if each Pod is healthy.</p><h3>Read replicas and sharding</h3><p>The repository contains optional reader datasource configuration, disabled in the shared application ConfigMap. That does not prove a replica is deployed or that every read is routed there. Replica lag makes read-after-write behavior a design decision. Sharding is not implemented; the reference discusses how a partition key, routing, migrations, and cross-shard queries would change the design.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="redis-runtime-walkthrough"><h3>Two names, two Redis processes, four main uses</h3><p><code>redis</code> and <code>redis-cache</code> are independent Kubernetes workloads. They use the same Redis image but different Services, credentials, consumers, and eviction policies. The browser never connects to either instance directly.</p>${makeTable(['Redis workload','Consumers','Stored state','Configured policy'],[['redis:6379','Gateway and Member Service','Gateway sessions, rate-limit tokens, and membership locks.','128 MB Redis maxmemory; noeviction.'],['redis-cache:6379','Society Service','Cached response data, negative entries, and cache-load locks.','128 MB Redis maxmemory; allkeys-lru.']])}<h4>Creation and connection</h4>${flow('Redis setup and connectivity',[['Kubernetes creates the workloads','Each Deployment starts one redis:7.4-alpine Pod. Its ClusterIP Service selects the matching app label and exposes port 6379.'],['Configuration chooses the address','Gateway and Member read REDIS_HOST=redis. Society reads SOCIETY_CACHE_REDIS_HOST=redis-cache. Kubernetes DNS resolves those names in the namespace.'],['Secrets supply credentials','The main instance reads redis-password; the cache instance reads redis-cache-password from emerald-security-credentials. Consumer Deployments receive the corresponding values.'],['Spring supplies the client integration','Gateway uses Spring Session and RedisRateLimiter. Member uses StringRedisTemplate. Society uses Spring Cache with a RedisCacheManager and explicit StringRedisTemplate operations.']])}<h4>1. A login session</h4><p>After credentials are validated, Gateway stores authenticated session state through Spring Session. The browser receives the <code>SESSION</code> cookie containing a session ID. Later requests use that ID to locate shared session state. Any compatible Gateway replica can use the same store. The configured session timeout defaults to 30 minutes; the namespace is <code>emerald:gateway:session</code>.</p><h4>2. A request token bucket</h4><p>Gateway evaluates a Redis-backed token bucket for the matched route and resolved caller key. Member routes default to a replenish rate of 10 tokens per second and a burst capacity of 20; Society routes default to 20 and 40. A request costs one token. A depleted bucket returns HTTP 429. The login limiter has its own configuration and key behavior.</p><h4>3. A membership write lock</h4><p>Member builds a key under <code>emerald:member:membership-lock:</code>. An atomic <code>setIfAbsent</code> stores an ownership token with an expiration. A competing request waits within the configured bound. Release checks the token before deleting the key. A process crash leaves a lease that can expire rather than an indefinite lock.</p><h4>4. A cached Society lookup</h4>${flow('Cache-aside request',[['Check the key','A cacheable service operation checks the dedicated cache Redis. Authorization still determines which operation and data are permitted.'],['Return a hit','If the entry exists, the cached representation avoids the corresponding database load.'],['Coordinate a miss','The cache wrapper attempts a per-key load lock. Waiters recheck for the result; after the bounded wait, a waiter may load without caching.'],['Load and store','The loader queries PostgreSQL and saves the representation with its configured TTL. Warmup can populate selected entries before the first request.'],['Invalidate after a write commits','The flat-change listener removes the affected flat entry, list entries, floor-specific entries, and negative entry after commit.']])}${makeTable(['Cache name','Content','Base TTL'],[['societyFloors','Floor list','10 minutes'],['societyFlatsByFloor','Flats on a floor','5 minutes'],['societyFlatById','One flat','2 minutes'],['societyFlats','All-flat list','5 minutes']])}<p>TTL jitter subtracts up to 30 seconds from the base expiration. A not-found marker has a separate 30-second base TTL. Load-lock defaults are a 2-second wait, a 30-second lease, and a 25-millisecond retry interval.</p><h4>Outages and consistency limits</h4><p>On cache Redis errors, Society can bypass Redis briefly and load the database under a per-Pod fallback limit. The shared configuration allows 16 concurrent fallback loads; overload is reported as <code>503 CACHE_FALLBACK_OVERLOADED</code>. This behavior applies to the disposable cache, not automatically to session state or membership coordination.</p>${note('Separate memory; separate consequences','Cache eviction does not remove sessions because the instances are separate. Both Deployments use emptyDir storage, so Pod replacement loses their data. noeviction prevents automatic memory-pressure eviction; it does not make Redis durable and can cause writes to fail when memory is exhausted.')}<p>After-commit invalidation avoids eviction for rolled-back writes. It does not eliminate every stale-read race or guarantee delivery of a failed invalidation. TTL bounds the lifetime of cached entries; more stringent consistency requires a stronger design.</p><p class="source-note">Start with <a href="kubernetes/config/application-config.yaml">the ConfigMap</a>, <a href="kubernetes/redis/deployment.yaml">main Redis Deployment</a>, <a href="kubernetes/redis-cache/deployment.yaml">cache Redis Deployment</a>, and the cache configuration and invalidation listener linked in the references.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-redis-resilience"><h3>Bound a dependency call before adding retries</h3><p>Member calls Society to validate a flat. An unavailable dependency should not consume all request threads, database connections, or caller patience. Each resilience control addresses a different part of that problem.</p>${makeTable(['Control','Purpose','Tradeoff to explain'],[['Connect timeout','Bounds establishing the connection.','A responsive connection can still return a slow response.'],['Response/read timeout','Bounds waiting at the configured client layer.','This is not necessarily the entire end-to-end deadline.'],['Retry','Repeats selected transient failures with bounded attempts and delay.','It increases downstream traffic and latency; unsafe writes need explicit replay guarantees.'],['Circuit breaker','Stops repeated calls while a dependency is failing, then probes recovery.','State is local to an instance unless a shared design is added.'],['Bulkhead','Caps concurrent dependency work.','Excess traffic must be rejected or handled within a bounded queue.'],['Idempotency','Recognizes supported request replays.','It does not make arbitrary operations safe to retry.']])}<h3>Explain one failure path</h3><p>If Society cannot be validated within the configured policy, Member should return the mapped dependency failure rather than inventing a valid flat. A circuit breaker reduces repeated calls during a continuing outage; a bulkhead protects the rest of the service from unbounded waiting. Cache fallback serves a different goal: it can bypass a disposable accelerator when the database remains available.</p><h3>Read the timeout chain</h3><p>The Gateway response timeout defaults to 28 seconds. The NGINX reference uses 32-second upstream read/send timeouts. Those are separate proxy wait limits, not a single deadline shared by every component. Budget authentication/session validation, network overhead, selected retries, and work performed while a transaction is open.</p><p>Measure physical dependency calls and rejected work. A successful retry can hide a degraded first attempt if you record only the final response.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-security-story"><h3>Identity, permission, and record scope are separate checks</h3><p>Gateway authenticates API requests; the backends enforce operation permissions and record-level scope. The current source permits authenticated <code>/api/v1/**</code> traffic at Gateway rather than restricting every business route to ADMIN. Backend access helpers evaluate grants such as <code>MEMBER_VIEW:SOCIETY</code>, <code>MEMBER_VIEW:SELF</code>, and <code>FLAT_VIEW:OWN_FLATS</code>.</p>${flow('Authenticated request',[['Establish the session','The caller obtains CSRF material and logs in. Member owns credential data; Gateway creates the shared Redis-backed session.'],['Validate the subsequent request','Gateway checks the session and applicable CSRF protection. The backend validates the supplied session through the trusted Gateway path rather than trusting caller-supplied identity headers.'],['Resolve current access','The request identity includes the society, member association, grants, and permitted flat IDs derived by the configured identity flow.'],['Authorize the operation and object','Service rules check both the permission and the requested member or flat. An authenticated resident does not receive unrestricted directory access.']])}<h3>What CSRF protects</h3><p>Session cookies are automatically sent by browsers, so state-changing cookie-authenticated requests need the expected CSRF material. The session cookie and CSRF token serve different purposes. A valid CSRF token does not grant a business permission, and a valid session does not bypass a record-scope check.</p><h3>Prevent IDOR at the record boundary</h3><p>Changing a UUID must not allow a caller to retrieve another member’s data or another flat’s history. Use two accounts with different permitted objects, then repeat the same operation with the other account’s identifier. Verify status, response content, and any resulting audit or business state—not only the response code.</p><h3>Revocation and sensitive state</h3><p>The current access model resolves permissions and associations through the identity validation flow so subsequent validated requests can reflect access changes. Revocation cannot undo a completed operation. Disabled roles, ended relationships, removed account grants, and member status changes each need tests for the relevant workflow.</p>${note('Use the current access model','Some older implementation-phase examples discuss ADMIN-only APIs. Their request bodies and infrastructure examples remain reference material; use the current permission and scope rules for expected access. Gateway metrics still have a separate ADMIN requirement.')}<h3>Protect the service path</h3><p>Internal service tokens protect credential and session-validation calls; they are not public user credentials. NetworkPolicy and the HTTPS edge help only when the relevant configuration is applied and enforced. Keep credentials out of page examples, URL parameters, and logs. Audit snapshots exclude credential fields but can still contain personal business data and require restricted access.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-production-story"><h3>Read the deployment as a chain of resources</h3>${flow('Kubernetes resource relationships',[['Image','A Docker image packages the application. Building an image does not by itself update a running Pod.'],['Namespace, configuration, and credentials','The namespace groups resources. ConfigMaps hold non-secret settings. Secrets supply private values referenced by the Deployments.'],['Deployment and Pod','The Deployment declares the Pod template and desired replica count. Pods receive labels, containers, environment variables, probes, and resource limits.'],['Service and DNS','A Service selects Pods by labels. Its stable DNS name is the application’s destination even when Pod IPs change.'],['Gateway and optional edge','The Spring Gateway routes business paths to backend Services. The NGINX Gateway API configuration provides the separate HTTPS edge when installed.']])}<h3>Why both replicas use the same database</h3><p>Two Society Pods both serve the same Society data. Giving them unrelated databases would divide the application state unless a separate replication or partitioning design existed. Service replicas scale execution; data ownership still belongs to the service.</p><h3>Health and shutdown</h3><p>Startup probes allow initialization time. Readiness controls whether a Pod should receive traffic. Liveness decides when to restart a container. Avoid turning a temporary remote dependency failure into repeated restarts of otherwise functioning applications. The source configures graceful shutdown with a 20-second shutdown-phase timeout; compare that with Pod termination grace and actual request duration.</p><h3>Scaling is a capacity change</h3><p>Replica count, CPU and memory limits, HTTP concurrency, Redis capacity, and database pools are connected. An HPA manifest expresses a policy; it does not prove metrics-server is available or the policy is active. Verify the installed resources and the capacity of stateful dependencies before claiming automatic scalability.</p><p>Use versioned images for reviewable rollouts. A Deployment rollback changes a retained Pod template; it does not reverse database migrations or independently changed configuration.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="operations-reading-guide"><h3>Use the right signal for the question</h3>${makeTable(['Question','Evidence to inspect','What it cannot prove alone'],[['Is this Pod ready?','Readiness, restart count, events, and rollout status.','Business correctness for every API flow.'],['Why did this request fail?','Correlation ID, application logs, dependency timings, and error response.','Fleet-wide error rate from one request.'],['Is the service under pressure?','Request rates, latency, errors, saturation, pools, cache metrics, and rejected work.','The cause of an individual failure without request context.'],['Which services were involved?','Distributed trace spans when configured and exported.','Complete coverage when sampling or export is disabled.'],['Did this business write commit?','Read-back state and the transaction’s audit record.','A guarantee inferred from a successful readiness probe.'],['Is the documented version deployed?','Image tags/digests, rollout state, and runtime verification.','Current deployment from a saved report.']])}<h3>Separate three kinds of statements</h3><dl class="definitions"><dt>Implemented in source</dt><dd>The current code or manifest contains the behavior. Deployment and runtime checks are still separate.</dd><dt>Recorded verification</dt><dd>A dated report captures a particular environment and application version. Re-run the relevant checks after changes.</dd><dt>Design extension</dt><dd>A proposed capability such as sharding, multi-society onboarding, or a stronger cross-service consistency mechanism.</dd></dl><h3>Use low-cardinality measurements</h3><p>Route names, operation names, and bounded outcomes make useful metric labels. Member IDs, session IDs, idempotency keys, and arbitrary request values create unbounded series and can expose sensitive context. Put an appropriate request identifier in logs or trace context instead.</p><h3>Discuss a design change with its cost</h3><p>A read replica can offload selected reads but introduces lag and routing rules. More service replicas add execution capacity but increase downstream connections. A cache reduces repeated reads but adds expiration, invalidation, and outage behavior. Stronger consistency may require serialized work, compensation, or a workflow redesign. Explain the requirement that justifies the extra mechanism.</p></section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="hands-on-sequence"><h3>Choose one runtime and verify what already exists</h3><p>Option A runs the Java applications locally; Option B runs them on Kubernetes with local access through Gateway port-forwarding. Read the detailed setup for the chosen option. Reuse a healthy environment and its credentials rather than treating reset commands as normal startup.</p><h4>Inspect the Kubernetes environment</h4>${code("kubectl --context docker-desktop get deployments,pods,services -n emerald-housing\nkubectl --context docker-desktop get events -n emerald-housing --sort-by=.lastTimestamp")}<p>These commands read the existing Docker Desktop cluster. They do not establish that database credentials, images, or optional edge resources are correct; use rollout status and the relevant logs to investigate failures.</p><h4>Connect to Gateway</h4>${code('kubectl --context docker-desktop port-forward service/api-gateway 8080:8080 -n emerald-housing')}<p>Keep that terminal open. In another terminal, check readiness:</p>${code('Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:8080/actuator/health/readiness')}<h4>Exercise a complete client workflow</h4><ol><li>Import the repository’s collection and environment. Supply private account values locally.</li><li>Obtain CSRF material, log in, and refresh CSRF material where the collection requires it.</li><li>Read one permitted record and inspect its representation and ETag.</li><li>Perform one authorized change, then read it back and inspect its audit history where permitted.</li><li>Repeat with a restricted identity and an out-of-scope identifier to verify the access boundary.</li><li>For a cache exercise, compare cold and repeated reads through available metrics; do not infer a cache hit from speed alone.</li></ol><h3>Keep verification focused</h3><p>Run the checks affected by a change. Source parsing validates an artifact; tests validate their assertions; a rollout validates deployment progress; live requests validate the exercised runtime behavior. Record the date, version, environment, request, and expected result so another person can reproduce the evidence.</p>${note('Commands are reference material','This page does not execute its examples. Destructive cleanup and rollback procedures in the detailed setup are exceptional recovery actions, not prerequisites for reading or verifying the system.')}</section>`,
`<section class="explanation" id="interview-answer-framework"><h3>Build an answer from a request, not a technology list</h3><p>Begin with the user and business operation. Name the owning service and data. Trace authentication, authorization, any dependency calls, the transaction, and the response. Then describe one failure, the protection for it, and the remaining limitation.</p><h3>Your 90-second project story</h3><blockquote>“Emerald Housing is scoped to one society. Society owns flats and building data; Member owns people, assignments, accounts, and access. Gateway provides the entry path. For a membership assignment, Member checks authority, validates the flat through Society, coordinates the write, and commits business and audit state. Redis supplies shared sessions and limits, membership locks, and a separate disposable response cache. The main tradeoffs are dependency availability, transaction duration, cache consistency, and database capacity.”</blockquote><h3>Practise in three rounds</h3>${makeTable(['Round','Exercise','Evidence of a strong answer'],[['5 minutes','Draw the services and trace a membership assignment.','Clear ownership, current permissions, and the actual transaction boundary.'],['10 minutes','Explain concurrent writes, cache misses, and a dependency outage.','Mechanism, reason, failure behavior, and a specific remaining limit.'],['15 minutes','Propose multi-society support or higher capacity.','Requirements first, followed by data changes, rollout compatibility, and measurements.']])}<h3>Follow-ups you should be able to answer</h3><ul><li>Why are there two Redis instances, and what happens if each is lost?</li><li>Why does a Redis lock still need database constraints?</li><li>How do you prevent one authenticated resident from reading another resident’s data?</li><li>What is the difference between an ETag, an idempotency key, and a correlation ID?</li><li>Why can increasing Pod replicas make the database slower?</li><li>Which statements describe code, which describe a verified run, and which are proposals?</li></ul><p>The detailed questions now sit beside their relevant topic: Redis scenarios in the Redis chapter, sharding and read replicas in Data, IDOR in Security, and rollout questions in Kubernetes. Use those sections for deeper practice.</p></section>`
];
